1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,826
Hi, and welcome to our full-stack AI
portfolio walkthrough.

2
00:00:03,826 --> 00:00:08,928
This project brings together a responsive
portfolio website and Deskwise, a working

3
00:00:08,928 --> 00:00:10,204
support assistant demo.

4
00:00:10,204 --> 00:00:17,006
The goal is to show a complete user journey,
from a polished interface to a backend

5
00:00:17,006 --> 00:00:18,706
API and persistent storage.

6
00:00:18,706 --> 00:00:24,658
The identity shown here is a placeholder,
and the two additional project cards are

7
00:00:24,658 --> 00:00:26,784
clearly marked as design concepts.

8
00:00:27,984 --> 00:00:29,948
Let’s open the AI playground.

9
00:00:29,948 --> 00:00:33,876
Deskwise answers questions about a fictional
store’s returns, shipping, account

10
00:00:33,876 --> 00:00:35,055
access, and billing.

11
00:00:35,055 --> 00:00:38,590
For this recording, we’re using the built-in
demo mode.

12
00:00:38,590 --> 00:00:42,912
It retrieves sample policies and returns
deterministic responses, without making a

13
00:00:42,912 --> 00:00:44,090
live model request.

14
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:50,375
When we ask how to return an order, the
assistant displays the relevant policy and a

15
00:00:50,375 --> 00:00:51,161
source reference.

16
00:00:51,161 --> 00:00:54,304
The conversation is saved through the Python
backend.

17
00:00:55,517 --> 00:01:00,945
Clicking the reference opens the knowledge
base, so we can inspect the policy behind

18
00:01:00,945 --> 00:01:01,720
the response.

19
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:06,372
This keeps the information visible instead
of presenting an answer without context.

20
00:01:06,372 --> 00:01:09,474
The project also includes an optional OpenAI
integration.

21
00:01:09,474 --> 00:01:14,901
With a valid API key configured securely on
the server, the backend sends selected

22
00:01:14,901 --> 00:01:18,390
policy excerpts and recent conversation
history to the model.

23
00:01:18,390 --> 00:01:22,267
That live integration is not being
demonstrated in this recording.

24
00:01:23,466 --> 00:01:26,041
Some questions need a human next step.

25
00:01:26,041 --> 00:01:31,558
In the support tickets panel, we can create
a ticket for a delayed delivery, then

26
00:01:31,558 --> 00:01:33,765
mark it resolved or reopen it.

27
00:01:33,765 --> 00:01:38,547
These are local demo tickets, not messages
sent to an actual support team.

28
00:01:38,547 --> 00:01:41,489
Refreshing the page shows that the record
persists.

29
00:01:41,489 --> 00:01:45,535
The database keeps each browser session’s
conversations and tickets separate, while

30
00:01:45,535 --> 00:01:48,846
the interface gives users clear feedback
about their actions.

31
00:01:50,045 --> 00:01:56,203
Under the hood, the application uses HTML,
CSS, and JavaScript on the frontend, Flask

32
00:01:56,203 --> 00:02:00,161
and Python for the API, and SQLite for
storage.

33
00:02:00,161 --> 00:02:04,559
It includes input validation, CSRF checks,
and parameterized database queries.

34
00:02:04,559 --> 00:02:10,717
Six backend tests passed, along with browser
checks of the core workflow and mobile

35
00:02:10,717 --> 00:02:11,157
layout.

36
00:02:11,157 --> 00:02:15,555
This is a portfolio demonstration, not a
production support platform.

37
00:02:15,555 --> 00:02:20,833
It provides a focused starting point for
discussing your product requirements and

38
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:22,592
building the next iteration.

39
00:02:22,592 --> 00:02:23,912
Thanks for watching.
